CAT = "mtguardDuties"; SEG = "DUT"
# Jobsplus Award in Private Security Guard Services, Module 1 outcomes (duties, legal terms, legislation
# applicable on duty, personal initiative); S.L. 389.01 First Schedule (functions and duties,
# attributes of a security person); Occupational Health and Safety Authority Act (Cap. 424) art. 7
# (duties of workers); GDPR art. 5 principles and the Data Protection Act (Cap. 586).
Q = [
("What is the main purpose of a private guard on a client's site?",
 "Deter, detect and report", ["Punish offenders", "Enforce council bye-laws", "Collect debts for the client"],
 "A guard prevents loss by being seen, spots problems early and reports them to the client and the police. Punishment belongs to the courts and bye-laws to community officers."),
("A guard starts at a new site in Birkirkara. What should he read before his first patrol?",
 "The site's written instructions", ["Last week's newspapers", "The client's tax return", "His old site's rota"],
 "Site instructions set out patrol routes, contacts, alarm procedures and access rules for that site, which differ from his previous post."),
("A client tells a guard to search staff lockers without their consent. What should he do?",
 "Decline and report it upward", ["Do it quietly at night", "Do it wearing gloves", "Ask a colleague to do it for him"],
 "No instruction can give a guard powers the law does not. Article 12(3) of Cap. 389 says a licence gives no rights over third parties' property, so he refers the request to his supervisor."),
("In legal terms, what is the 'suspect'?",
 "A person thought to have offended", ["A person already proven guilty in court", "Any visitor to the site", "The victim of the crime"],
 "A suspect is someone believed, on some grounds, to have committed an offence. Guilt is decided only by a court."),
("In legal terms, what is a 'witness'?",
 "Someone who saw or heard events", ["Someone accused of the crime", "The police officer in charge of the case", "The lawyer for the victim"],
 "A witness gives evidence of what he personally saw, heard or did. A guard is often a witness and may be called to court."),
("In legal terms, what is 'evidence'?",
 "Material that can prove a fact", ["Any rumour heard on site", "The guard's view of guilt", "Whatever the client wants"],
 "Evidence is anything, such as a statement, an object, a document or a recording, that helps a court decide whether a fact is true."),
("What is a guard's 'duty of care' to people on site?",
 "To avoid foreseeable harm", ["To obey every visitor", "To mind visitors' cars free", "To lend visitors his phone"],
 "Duty of care means acting with reasonable care so that people on the premises are not harmed by what the guard does or fails to do, such as leaving an exit locked."),
("A guard finds water leaking onto a stairway, making it slippery. What should he do?",
 "Warn people and report it", ["Leave it for the cleaners", "Mop it and say nothing", "Close the building"],
 "Article 7 of the Occupational Health and Safety Authority Act (Cap. 424) makes every worker safeguard his own and others' health and safety and co-operate with the employer. Warning people and reporting the leak lets the employer deal with it."),
("Why must a guard wear the safety equipment his employer gives him?",
 "Workers must use it properly", ["It makes him look official", "Only when OHSA inspectors are visiting", "It is optional for guards"],
 "Article 7 of Cap. 424 makes every worker safeguard his own health and safety and co-operate with the employer on safety matters, which includes using the protective equipment provided as instructed."),
("A guard feels very tired on a long night shift. What is the right step?",
 "Tell his supervisor", ["Sleep in the client's car", "Leave the post early", "Take strong pills unseen"],
 "A guard who cannot stay alert is a risk to himself and the site. Telling the supervisor lets the relief be arranged; leaving or sleeping on post breaks his duties."),
("Which of these shows sensible personal initiative on patrol?",
 "Closing an unlatched fire door", ["Rewiring a faulty light", "Moving a suspect package", "Lending his master keys to a cleaner"],
 "Initiative means acting within one's role to stop a problem growing: securing the door and recording it. Electrical repairs, handling suspect items and lending keys are outside the role."),
("Under data protection law, how should a guard treat names and ID numbers in the visitor log?",
 "Only for site security", ["Share them with friends", "Post them on social media", "Sell them to marketers"],
 "The GDPR's purpose limitation principle says personal data collected for one purpose must not be used for another. Malta's Data Protection Act (Cap. 586) applies the GDPR here."),
("A caller says he is from the police and asks for a staff member's home address. What should the guard do?",
 "Refer him to the supervisor", ["Read the address out", "Text it to the caller", "Read it out if the caller sounds genuine"],
 "A guard cannot confirm who a caller is by phone. Requests for personal data go through the employer or client, who can verify them and apply data protection law."),
("Which body supervises data protection in Malta?",
 "The IDPC", ["The MFSA", "The MCCAA", "Transport Malta"],
 "The Information and Data Protection Commissioner (IDPC) supervises the GDPR and the Data Protection Act (Cap. 586) in Malta."),
("Why should a guard keep site security details to himself off duty?",
 "Leaks help criminals plan", ["It is never a problem", "Only alarm codes matter", "Clients enjoy hearing gossip"],
 "Patrol times, alarm settings and weak points are valuable to burglars. Keeping them confidential is part of the guard's duty to the client."),
("A friend offers a guard money to leave a side gate unlocked. What should he do?",
 "Refuse and report it", ["Take it once", "Leave the gate, keep quiet", "Ask for more"],
 "Taking a bribe would make the guard part of a crime and could lead to his licence being withdrawn under article 13 of Cap. 389. He refuses and reports the approach."),
("A guard is found guilty of theft from a client. What may follow for his licence?",
 "It may be withdrawn", ["Nothing, it is separate", "Only a pay cut", "It is extended"],
 "Article 13(1) of Cap. 389 lets the Commissioner withdraw or suspend a licence where facts arise that would have justified refusing it, and a theft conviction is such a fact under article 10."),
("Which attribute does the official syllabus list for a good security person?",
 "Alertness and good judgement", ["A loud, commanding voice", "A tall, heavy build", "Fluency in at least five languages"],
 "The First Schedule of S.L. 389.01 lists the attributes of a security person, physical and mental qualities, creating effective awareness and good judgement among its topics."),
("A guard is asked to make a decision he is not sure is lawful. What is the best course?",
 "Ask the supervisor first", ["Guess and act quickly", "Do nothing and say nothing", "Ask a visitor's opinion"],
 "When a guard is unsure of his powers, checking with his supervisor or calling the police avoids an unlawful act that could lead to prosecution under article 86 of the Criminal Code."),
("Why should a guard arrive on time for a handover?",
 "A post must never be left empty", ["The relief is paid from that minute", "Only for the logbook", "It does not really matter"],
 "A late relief leaves the outgoing guard unable to leave or the site unguarded. A proper handover passes on keys, incidents and open issues."),
("At a handover, what should the outgoing guard pass on?",
 "Incidents, keys and open issues", ["Only the weather forecast", "His views on the staff", "Nothing, since the log says it all"],
 "A full handover briefs the incoming guard on what happened, what is still open and what equipment and keys are being handed over, and both sign the log."),
("What is meant by 'acting within your remit'?",
 "Doing only what your role allows", ["Doing what you feel is right", "Doing what visitors ask", "Doing a police officer's job when needed"],
 "A guard's remit is set by the law, his licence category and his site instructions. Going beyond it, such as searching without consent, can be unlawful."),
("A guard uses the client's phone line for long personal calls. Which duty does he breach?",
 "His duty to the client", ["No duty, it is a perk", "A traffic regulation", "A local council bye-law"],
 "The guard is trusted with the client's property and time. Personal use of equipment breaches that trust and can tie up lines needed in an emergency."),
("What should a guard do if his instructions conflict with a fire safety rule?",
 "Follow safety, report it", ["Follow the instruction", "Ask the next visitor", "Ignore both and carry on"],
 "No site instruction can override safety law. Keeping an exit clear protects lives; the conflict is then reported so the instructions can be corrected."),
]
